"""Outlines of SHMÚ's 72 warning regions, for CAP alerts that carry no polygon.

SHMÚ issues each alert for one warning region: a district (okres), with the
city districts of Bratislava and of Košice merged into one region each. An
alert names its region by a Meteoalarm ``EMMA_ID`` geocode and usually also
carries the region's outline as a ``<polygon>``, which the parser prefers.
When the polygon is missing, the parser takes the outline from this table.
Without it the alert would have no geometry, and an alert without geometry
covers the whole country.

Verified 2026-11-26 over the whole CAP archive (21 days, 1259 alerts): every
alert has exactly one area with an ``EMMA_ID``, and all 72 regions appear.
Piešťany (SK204) never sends a polygon, and the other 71 always do, identical
in all their alerts. The daily live test fails when an alert names a region
that is missing here.

Provenance - regenerate from the district map that SHMÚ's own warnings page
(https://www.shmu.sk/sk/?page=987) draws::

    # https://www.shmu.sk/assets/maps/meteo_map.json
    #   GeoJSON FeatureCollection, one Polygon per region, with the
    #   properties ID, SKRATKA and NAZOV_OKRE.
    # ID is not the EMMA_ID number (Bratislava is 101 but SK100, the Košice
    # region's 80x are SK12x). Take each feature's EMMA_ID from a CAP alert
    # whose <identifier> ends in "_<SKRATKA>" ("..._PN" for Piešťany).
    # Vertices are (lon, lat): store them as CAP does, "lat,lon lat,lon ...",
    # rounded to four decimals (under 6 m), dropping a vertex that rounds to
    # the same point as the one before it.
    # Only Košice okolie has a hole, the city of Košice. A CAP polygon cannot
    # express one, so splice the hole into the outer ring through a
    # zero-width cut between their two closest vertices. The even-odd test
    # in Warning.covers() then leaves the hole out.

The map traces the boundaries a little differently from the CAP polygons: a
CAP vertex lies a median of 90 m (p90 190 m) from the map's boundary of the
same region. The one large difference is around Kežmarok, whose CAP polygon
takes in about 200 km² that the map gives to Stará Ľubovňa, Levoča and
Sabinov. So while a region's polygon is missing, a point near its boundary
can get its warnings when it should not, or miss them. Every station and
rain gauge lies in exactly one region of this table.
"""

